 ### [Symmetry Mapping](869-symmetry-mapping.md)
-Reduces the 256 configurations to 23 base cases and 24 rotations, requiring only one lookup for base topology and a vertex permutation [869-symmetry-mapping]. Choose when memory is constrained and rotational symmetry can be exploited.
+Symmetry Mapping reduces the 256 possible binary configurations into a smaller set of canonical base cases by exploiting the geometric symmetries of the cube [869-symmetry-mapping]. Instead of storing a unique triangle connection table for all 256 cases, it maps each configuration to one of 23 unique base cases and a specific rotation [869-symmetry-mapping]. This simplifies the triangle generation logic by requiring only one lookup for the base topology and a transformation for the vertex indices [869-symmetry-mapping]. It builds on [Lookup Table and Configuration](4978-lookup-table-and-configuration.md).
 
 ### [Marching Cubes Lookup Table](4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table.md)
-The standard static 256-entry table mapping cases to fixed triangulations, prioritizing $O(1)$ speed over topological correctness at saddle points [4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table]. Choose for general-purpose rendering where performance is critical and ambiguity artifacts are acceptable or handled externally.
+The Marching Cubes Lookup Table (MC LUT) is a precomputed data structure that maps the 256 possible binary configurations of a voxel's eight corner signs relative to an isovalue to a specific triangulation of the isosurface within that voxel [4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table]. It solves the problem of determining which edges of a cube are intersected by the isosurface and how to connect those intersection points into triangles without runtime branching logic for topology [4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table]. Unlike sibling methods that may use symmetry mapping to reduce table size [Symmetry Mapping](869-symmetry-mapping.md) or dynamic ambiguity resolution [Improved Marching Cubes Algorithm](9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm.md), the standard MC LUT relies on a fixed, static configuration for all 256 cases, including ambiguous ones, prioritizing O(1) lookup speed over topological correctness at saddle points [4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table].
 
 ### [Watt & Watt Marching Cubes](7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes.md)
-Optimizes the standard LUT approach with vertex deduplication, modified ambiguity handling, and I/O buffering for large datasets [7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes]. Choose for large-scale volume rendering exceeding main memory capacity.
+Watt & Watt Marching Cubes (WWMC) is an optimized implementation of the Marching Cubes algorithm designed to address topological inconsistencies, redundant vertex computation, and I/O bottlenecks in large-scale volume rendering [7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes]. It distinguishes itself from the standard [Marching Cubes Lookup Table](4800-marching-cubes-lookup-table.md) by integrating a modified lookup table for consistent triangulation, vertex deduplication via auxiliary data structures, and dynamic I/O buffering to handle datasets exceeding main memory capacity [7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes]. Unlike [Improved Marching Cubes Algorithm](9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm.md) which may focus on specific geometric corrections, WWMC provides a holistic system-level optimization for arbitrary datasets [7787-watt-watt-marching-cubes].
 
 ### [Improved Marching Cubes Algorithm](9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm.md)
-Expands the configuration set to 24 cases and uses tunable interpolation to reduce voids and computational cost [9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm]. Choose for medical imaging where topological voids in ambiguous cases must be avoided and performance can be tuned via interpolation parameters.
+The Improved Marching Cubes (IMC) algorithm is a variant of the standard Marching Cubes (MC) method designed to enhance computational efficiency and topological robustness in 3D reconstruction, particularly for medical imaging [9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm]. It addresses two primary limitations of the standard approach: the generation of topological voids in ambiguous configurations and the high computational cost of linear interpolation and dense voxel traversal [9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm]. By expanding the topological configuration set from 15 to 24 cases, employing a tunable interpolation scheme (or midpoint selection), and utilizing optimized voxel representations, IMC achieves faster reconstruction speeds with minimal loss in geometric accuracy [9090-improved-marching-cubes-algorithm].
